# tests/assertions/wordb_engattempt.rxt — module `assertions`, [M6.2] WAVE B:
# `\b`/`\B` under the ENG_ATTEMPT (computed-goto) engine — every block here
# has a leading `^`, which routes the pattern off the table-driven DFA.
#
# ONE OF FOUR SHARDS wordb.rxt was split into 2026-08-21 (see
# wordb_basic.rxt's header for the split rationale and
# tests/assertions/CLAUDE.md for the full list). Every case here moved
# verbatim from wordb.rxt; nothing was added, removed, or reworded.
#
# THE SHARED CONTEXT, restated briefly because it governs every block below
# (full version: wordb_basic.rxt's header). EVERY EXPECTATION WAS PRODUCED BY
# libpcre2 10.46 (through tests/fuzz/pcre2_oracle, options = 0), never
# written by hand, and cross-checked by python3 `re` with 0 divergences.
# `\b`/`\B` are the module's first CONTEXT assertions, so the `ms`/`ns` cells
# below at `startpos > 0` are exercising mechanism 4 (assertions_design.md
# §3.8) a second time, on ENG_ATTEMPT's own accept shape — "BAKED INTO THE
# LABEL BODY rather than read from an array" per the section header just
# below, which is why a corpus with no `^` cells would exercise neither
# §3.6's table nor §3.8's seed on this engine at all.
#
# THIS SHARD CARRIES THE THREE K28-REINSTATED CELLS the wave B header names
# (`^\Bfoo`, `^\Bo`, `^a\bb`) — see the section comment immediately below for
# the full account; docs/dev/known_issues.md K28 is the closed issue.

# ====================================================================
# THE ENG_ATTEMPT HALF. A `^` routes the pattern to the
# computed-goto engine, whose accept is BAKED INTO THE LABEL
# BODY rather than read from an array -- so §3.6's table and
# §3.8's seed both take a different emitted shape there, and
# a corpus with no `^` cells would exercise neither.
# ====================================================================

# THREE ANCHORED CELLS WERE ABSENT FROM THIS BLOCK AND ARE NOW
# BACK -- `^\Bfoo`, `^\Bo` and `^a\bb`, reinstated 2026-08-19
# by the [M6.2] repair slice, which fixed the defect that kept
# them out. They are patterns that can NEVER match (their whole
# DFA is one dead state) and pcrec always answered them
# correctly; what failed was the C it emitted, which did not
# survive `-Wall -Wextra -Werror`: gcc inlined the
# always-returns-0 `<prefix>_search` into `<prefix>_match` and
# then reported the wrapper's `caps` array as
# maybe-uninitialized, even though the `found != 1`
# short-circuit means it is never read. That was KNOWN ISSUE
# K28 (base-tier reproducer `^a^b`, `-O1`-ONLY), CLOSED by
# initializing the wrapper's `caps` -- the emitter change is in
# src/gen/emit_dfa.c and its comment records why restructuring
# the test was measured NOT to work.
#
# IT WAS NEVER THIS WAVE'S DEFECT, which is why the three cells
# are ordinary members of this block now rather than a special
# section: `\b`/`\B` merely spelled a shape the base tier
# already reached. The LIVE anchored equivalents that stood in
# for them (`^\bfoo`, `^f\Bo`, `^a\b.`, `^\Bfoo|^x`,
# `^a\bb|^z`) STAY -- they carry the same impossibility on a
# sibling branch that keeps the automaton alive, which is a
# different emitted shape and its own coverage.
# K28 (reinstated). Impossible under `^`: position 0 is a boundary when `s[0]` is a word byte, so a
# leading `\B` before `f` can never hold.
pattern ^\Bfoo
features assertions
n ""
n "a"
n "x"
n " "
n "_"
n "1"
n "foo"
n "xfoo"
n " foo"
n "foo "
n "foox"
n "xfoofoo"
n "xfoo foo"
n "a x"
n "ab"
n "a b"
n "_a"
n "a_"
n "1a"
n "a1"
n "aa bb cc"
n "ab_cd ef"
n ".a."
n "a.b"
n "foobar"
n "a-b"
n "a\nb"
n "foo\n"
ns 0 "xfoo"
ns 1 "xfoo"
ns 2 "xfoo"
ns 3 "xfoo"
ns 4 "xfoo"
ns 0 " foo"
ns 1 " foo"
ns 2 " foo"
ns 3 " foo"
ns 4 " foo"
ns 0 "xfoo foo"
ns 1 "xfoo foo"
ns 2 "xfoo foo"
ns 3 "xfoo foo"
ns 4 "xfoo foo"
ns 5 "xfoo foo"
ns 6 "xfoo foo"
ns 7 "xfoo foo"
ns 8 "xfoo foo"
ns 0 "xfoofoo"
ns 1 "xfoofoo"
ns 2 "xfoofoo"
ns 3 "xfoofoo"
ns 4 "xfoofoo"
ns 5 "xfoofoo"
ns 6 "xfoofoo"
ns 7 "xfoofoo"
ns 0 "foo"
ns 1 "foo"
ns 2 "foo"
ns 3 "foo"
ns 0 "a x"
ns 1 "a x"
ns 2 "a x"
ns 3 "a x"
ns 0 "ab_cd ef"
ns 1 "ab_cd ef"
ns 2 "ab_cd ef"
ns 3 "ab_cd ef"
ns 4 "ab_cd ef"
ns 5 "ab_cd ef"
ns 6 "ab_cd ef"
ns 7 "ab_cd ef"
ns 8 "ab_cd ef"

